WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.160
<v Instructor>So now let's talk about AppSync.</v>

2
00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:03.810
So AppSync is a managed service

3
00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:05.970
that uses GraphQL in the backend.

4
00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.989
So anytime you see GraphQL, think AppSync.

5
00:00:08.989 --> 00:00:10.020
And GraphQL, what is it?

6
00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.150
Well, it makes it easy for applications

7
00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.160
to get exactly the data they need.

8
00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.620
That includes if you wanna combine data from one

9
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:17.730
or more sources of data.

10
00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.733
So it could be a NoSQL data store, a Relational database,

11
00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:24.480
HTTP APIs, also has integration with Dynamo DB,

12
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.310
Aurora, Elasticsearch and others.

13
00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:29.850
And you can even get data from custom sources using Lambda.

14
00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:32.850
The idea is that this data is going to be retrieved together

15
00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:33.990
and sent back to the clients.

16
00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:37.560
And the particularity of AppSync is that the data

17
00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.410
can be retrieved in real time with WebSockets

18
00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:42.510
or MQTT on WebSockets.

19
00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:44.190
So this real time aspect is very big

20
00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:46.650
and something that the exam may test you on.

21
00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.080
So for mobile applications, AppSync also gives you

22
00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.690
local data access and data synchronization.

23
00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:54.060
And just to get started, you need to upload one

24
00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.190
GraphQL schema, and off you go.

25
00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:57.630
So how does AppSync work?

26
00:00:57.630 --> 00:00:59.580
Well, you have AppSync in here, and your clients

27
00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.800
are accessing AppSync, very important,

28
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.590
mobile apps, web apps, realtime dashboards,

29
00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:06.870
or even for offline synchronizations.

30
00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:09.900
Within AppSync, you have GraphQL schema and then Resolvers.

31
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:13.050
And these resolvers know how to get the data

32
00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:14.880
from the various sources of data.

33
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:18.420
So it could be DynamoDB, Aurora, ElasticSearch Service,

34
00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.010
Lambda, which leads you to pretty much

35
00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:21.420
any kind of source you want

36
00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:25.260
or HTTP to lead to public HTTP APIs.

37
00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.510
Finally, of course, you monitor AppSync

38
00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:29.340
with CloudWatch Metrics and Logs.

39
00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:32.250
So from an exam perspective, AppSync is going to

40
00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.770
be used whenever you see GraphQL or whenever you need

41
00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.550
to have access to realtime data as well for an API.

42
00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.670
Next, we have an integration between AppSync and Cognito.

43
00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:44.880
So we can perform authorization on Cognito users

44
00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.160
based on the groups that they belong to.

45
00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:50.580
So in the GraphQL schema, you specify the security

46
00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.920
of your Cognito groups, just like this.

47
00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:56.580
So you have a type and you have @aws_auth,

48
00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.980
and then you define Cognito groups of bloggers and readers.

49
00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:02.130
So, both bloggers and readers can do a query.

50
00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:04.650
But on the type mutation to add a post,

51
00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:07.290
only bloggers can actually add a post,

52
00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:09.840
because they are authorized to do so.

53
00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.450
So from a solution architecture perspective,

54
00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:15.900
the client is going to authenticate with Cognito,

55
00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:20.640
get a JWT token, and pass hat JSON Web token

56
00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.410
into AppSync.

57
00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:24.300
Then AppSync is going to verify the token

58
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.330
and make sure it's authorized with Cognito.

59
00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:29.010
And then as you can see in the resolvers,

60
00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.380
that I wrote right here, in your GraphQL schema,

61
00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:34.560
the resolvers are going to look at the group

62
00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.290
that the user in Cognito belongs to.

63
00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.410
And then if it's all good, then access, for example,

64
00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:44.370
your backend, it could be a DynamoDB database.

65
00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:45.810
Okay, so that's it.

66
00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.760
I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

